//! The layers a board is built from.

import std::parts::passive::Color

/// What a board is built from: how many copper layers, how thick the finished board is, and
/// how heavy the copper is. A manufacturer's process fixes some of these and leaves the
/// rest for the board to choose.
interface Stackup {
    /// Copper layers.
    .layers: Number
    /// The finished board, copper and mask included.
    .thickness: Length
    /// Finished outer copper, in ounces per square foot.
    .copper_weight: Number

    // How a board of more than two layers is built up. A two-layer process leaves these
    // unstated: its one core is what the thickness leaves after the copper. A process with
    // inner layers states every one (ADR 0111).

    /// The name the fabricator gives the build-up, such as `JLC04161H-7628`.
    .build: String
    /// The copper of an inner layer.
    .inner_copper: Length
    /// The dielectric layers between the copper layers, from the top: `.dielectric1` is
    /// between the top copper and the first inner layer, and a board of n layers has n - 1.
    /// Nine are declared, for up to ten layers; a process states those it has.
    .dielectric1: DielectricLayer
    .dielectric2: DielectricLayer
    .dielectric3: DielectricLayer
    .dielectric4: DielectricLayer
    .dielectric5: DielectricLayer
    .dielectric6: DielectricLayer
    .dielectric7: DielectricLayer
    .dielectric8: DielectricLayer
    .dielectric9: DielectricLayer

    // What the fabricator's impedance calculator puts over and into an outer track (issue
    // #1007). A process states them where its calculator's page does and leaves them out
    // otherwise; a process that states none gets the track impedance of its outer layers
    // without a solder mask and with the copper weight's nominal thickness.

    /// The solder mask over the dielectric layer, beside an outer track: JLCPCB's "coating
    /// above substrate".
    .impedance_mask_thickness: Length optional
    /// The relative permittivity of the solder mask.
    .impedance_mask_epsilon: Number optional
    /// The thickness the calculator gives an outer track's copper, which is the finished
    /// copper with its plating, and not the nominal thickness of the copper weight.
    .impedance_outer_copper: Length optional

    /// The colour of the solder mask, both faces (issue #771): a variant of the same `Color` an
    /// LED's `.color` is, and a board states it, `.process.stackup.mask_colour &= Color::Black`.
    /// It is a choice of the board, not a rule of the process, so no process states it; a board
    /// that does not is drawn and written as before (the 3D view's own mask tone, KiCad's
    /// green). The 3D view, `view 3d`, `dev board3d`'s glTF file and the board file's stackup draw
    /// and write the mask in it, and `order.txt` names it. A colour the fabricator does not
    /// offer is the board's to check against its order form.
    .mask_colour: Color optional
}

/// One dielectric layer of a build-up, the dielectric between two copper layers, as the
/// fabricator's build-up table gives it: one prepreg or core, stated in the four fields below,
/// or several plies pressed together (two sheets of prepreg, or a prepreg, a core with no copper
/// and a prepreg), stated each in a `.plyN` from the top and not in the four fields. A layer
/// states one or the other; the design stage adds up the plies' thicknesses and takes their
/// permittivity in series (`netsmith_core::DielectricLayer::of_plies`).
interface DielectricLayer {
    /// `"prepreg"` or `"core"`.
    .kind: String
    /// The glass style of a prepreg as the table names it (`"7628"`, `"3313"`, `"2116"`); a
    /// core states none.
    .glass: String
    .thickness: Length
    /// The relative permittivity.
    .epsilon: Number
    /// The plies of a layer pressed from several, from the top: `.ply1` lies on the copper
    /// above. Four are declared, the most JLCPCB's impedance page puts between two copper
    /// layers.
    .ply1: DielectricPly
    .ply2: DielectricPly
    .ply3: DielectricPly
    .ply4: DielectricPly
}

/// One ply of a dielectric layer pressed from several: a sheet of prepreg, or a core with no
/// copper on it, with the fields of a one-ply `DielectricLayer`.
interface DielectricPly {
    /// `"prepreg"` or `"core"`.
    .kind: String
    /// The glass style of a prepreg; a core states none.
    .glass: String
    .thickness: Length
    /// The relative permittivity.
    .epsilon: Number
}

/// An inner copper layer: a plane or a signal layer (ADR 0111). A process with inner layers
/// has one for each (`.inner1`, `.inner2`..., counted from the top), and the board says what
/// each is, because only a board names nets (ADR 0005).
///
/// A plane is one sheet of copper on a net, a power or ground plane: the board names the net
/// (`.process.inner1.net &= "GND"`). A signal layer has no net; the board says it is one
/// (`.process.inner2.use &= "signal"`), and it is written as a signal layer and plotted and
/// checked like the outer layers. The router draws tracks on it as on the outer layers, joined
/// by through vias (issue #833); a net with a known current is drawn there at the width its
/// current needs in the inner copper (`.stackup.inner_copper`), which is wider than outside.
interface InnerLayer {
    /// `"plane"`, the default, or `"signal"`.
    .use: String
    .use ?= "plane"
    /// The net of a plane.
    .net: String
}

/// A copper pour on an outer layer: copper filled round the tracks and joined to its net,
/// the member `.top` or `.bottom` of a process. Unlike a plane (an `InnerLayer`) it leaves
/// room for the tracks and for copper of other nets, and it is optional: an outer layer whose
/// pour has no net has no pour. The board names the net (`.process.top.net &= "GND"`), because only
/// a board names nets (ADR 0005). Where two layers carry the same net, vias stitch them.
///
/// Why a member of the process beside the planes (the owner's decision, 2026-11-02): the net
/// of each inner layer is already stated there (ADR 0012), so the outer layers are said in the
/// same place, as `.top` and `.bottom`. Rejected: a board member that names its layer
/// (`.layer &= "top"`: two places would say what is on a layer, the layer would be a string
/// checked only by hand, and two pours on one layer would have to be refused by hand) and a
/// plane that may be poured (one type with two meanings, a whole layer with nothing routed
/// and a fill round the routing, so the router would have to ask which). An inner plane with
/// no net is an error, because a board has to do something with it (or say it is a signal
/// layer, ADR 0111); an outer pour with no net is no pour. Thermal relief is KiCad's default
/// unless the process states its gap and spoke width (`.thermal_gap`, `.spoke_width`, the
/// owner's decision, 2026-11-15), which follow the range convention; the planes keep the default.
/// A pour has the other settings of a zone the routes file draws by hand (`netsmith routes
/// add-zone`) too: its clearance, its least width and how it joins its net's pads (`.clearance`,
/// `.min_width`, `.pads`, issue #1084), so that a board whose source states its pour need not
/// draw a hand zone beside it to tune them.
interface Pour {
    /// The net the layer is poured on.
    .net: String
    /// The most space between the stitching vias that join the pour to the other layers of
    /// its net, in mm. The tool uses the top of the range: `.stitch_pitch &= 0mm..8mm`
    /// puts a via at least every 8 mm. Unset means the pads' own vias only.
    .stitch_pitch: Length
    /// The gap of a thermal relief, the space between a pad and the pour round it, in mm. The
    /// tool uses the bottom of the range, the least the process allows: `.thermal_gap &= 0.3mm..`
    /// leaves 0.3 mm. Unset means KiCad's default, 0.5 mm. The Gerbers, the design rules check
    /// and KiCad's zone all take it.
    .thermal_gap: Length
    /// The width of the spokes of a thermal relief, in mm. The tool uses the bottom of the
    /// range: `.spoke_width &= 0.3mm..` leaves 0.3 mm. Unset means KiCad's default, 0.5 mm.
    .spoke_width: Length
    /// The space between the pour and copper of other nets, in mm. The tool uses the bottom of
    /// the range: `.clearance &= 0.3mm..` leaves 0.3 mm. Unset means the space the process
    /// keeps round a plane: its `.min_clearance`, or its space to a hole in an inner layer if
    /// that is more. A bottom under the process's `.min_clearance` is an error.
    .clearance: Length
    /// The narrowest copper the pour keeps, in mm: its fill drops what is narrower (KiCad's
    /// minimum thickness of a zone). The tool uses the bottom of the range:
    /// `.min_width &= 0.3mm..` leaves 0.3 mm. Unset means 0.25 mm. A bottom under the process's
    /// `.min_track` is an error. A width over `.spoke_width` drops the spokes of the reliefs.
    .min_width: Length
    /// How the pour joins the pads of its net: `PadConnection::Thermal` (the default),
    /// `Solid`, `None` or `ThruHoleOnly`.
    .pads: PadConnection
}

/// How a pour or a zone joins the pads of its net (issue #1084): the `.pads` of a `Pour`,
/// `.process.top.pads &= PadConnection::Solid`. The routes file and `netsmith routes add-zone
/// --pads` spell the same four `thermal`, `solid`, `none` and `thru_hole_only` (the command
/// line `thru-hole-only`); KiCad's zone writes them in its `connect_pads`.
enum PadConnection {
    /// A thermal relief: a gap round the pad (`.thermal_gap`), crossed by four spokes
    /// (`.spoke_width`). It keeps the heat of soldering in the pad.
    Thermal,
    /// Solid copper up to the pad: the most current and heat, and a pad that is harder to solder.
    Solid,
    /// Not joined: the pad keeps the pour's clearance, as another net's pad would, and has to be
    /// joined to its net by a track.
    None,
    /// A thermal relief round a through-hole pad, solid copper up to a surface pad.
    ThruHoleOnly,
}
